Patches applied to vendored PUC Lua at build time
=================================================

third_party/lua stays byte-identical to the upstream release tarball. mk/lua.mk
copies the affected source into $(BUILD)/patched/lua, applies the patch there
with `patch -F0` (exact context, no fuzz) and compiles the copy instead of the
original. If a patch does not apply, the build fails; it never falls back to
the unpatched file.

Applies to: Lua 5.4.9 (third_party/lua/src). Re-check every patch on each Lua
upgrade: a clean apply is necessary, not sufficient. New loops over the subject
or pattern in lstrlib.c must be charged as well, and the sandbox tests in
t/sandbox (test_patterns) must still fault within budget.

0001-lstrlib-match-step-budget.patch
    Why: Lua pattern matching (string.find/match/gmatch/gsub) runs entirely in
    C, where the sandbox count hook never fires; a backtracking pattern such as
    ('a-'):rep(12) .. 'b' over a 20000-byte subject runs practically forever and
    would block the owner thread. Ticket canlin#7; the owner decided to keep
    lstrlib and add a step counter rather than replace the matcher.
    What: a per-MatchState step counter, incremented on every match() entry and
    tail-iteration, in max_expand/min_expand, the %b scan, back-reference
    comparisons (by compared length) and the plain-find candidate loop (by
    needle length). Every CANLIN_LSTR_CHUNK (256) steps the counter is passed
    to the host hook
        void canlin_lstr_charge(lua_State *L, size_t steps);
    defined in src/sandbox/sandbox.c. It charges the callback budget and raises
    the sandbox's sticky, uncatchable fault when the budget is exceeded; it
    ignores Lua states not created by the sandbox. gsub/gmatch loops across the
    subject are covered because each position enters match().
    Not covered: work below one chunk per call (bounded by 255 steps and
    accounted for by the calling VM instructions), luaL_Buffer copies of gsub
    output (bounded by the memory limit), and nospecials()'s strpbrk scan of the
    pattern (linear in the pattern length).
